<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=grenade">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hand grenade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1044">1044</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as grenade</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A grenade is a small explosive projectile thrown by hand or launched from a rifle attachment or launcher. The Chinese military book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wujing-zongyao">Wujing Zongyao</a> of 1044 describes iron shells packed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a> regarded as the prototype of the hand grenade. Catalan troops used grenades at the siege of Bonifacio in 1420, and in 1433 a fleet carried 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> pomegranates filled with gunpowder. Grenadiers appeared in European armies by the mid-17th century. The word is first recorded for explosives at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> in 1536.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grenade.jpg" alt="Hand grenade" width="330" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J-L Dubois · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MkII_07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1044</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Song China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Rudimentary incendiary grenades, jars of Greek fire, appeared in the Byzantine Empire not long after the reign of Leo III (717–741). The Wujing Zongyao's gunpowder shells are named only as a prototype, in Needham's view.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire<span>c. 672</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder<span>808</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons-in-world-war-i">Chemical weapons<span>August 1914</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=grenade">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
